# Cascade — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's
`reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following.

## The screen

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `howto`, `playing`, and `won`.
- The selected item on the menu the current screen shows, reported as
  `menuIndex`.
- The title entry last confirmed, reported as `titleIndex`. It is `0` until a
  title item is activated, and returning to the title selects that entry again,
  as `specs/screens.md` states.

## The thirteen piles

- The stock, the waste, each of the four foundations, and each of the seven
  columns, every one of them an ordered list of cards from its bottom card to its
  top card.
- Each card: a distinct id, its suit, its rank, and whether it is face-up.
- The waste's set memory: the number of cards on each set the stock turned, from
  the oldest set to the newest, as `specs/stock.md` states.

## The pointer and the hand

- The run currently in hand, or nothing when nothing is held. A held run carries
  its cards in order from the leading card, the pile and slot it was lifted from,
  and the top-left of its leading card.
- The pile the held run would land on, when there is one.
- The pointer's position and whether it is down.
- The most recent press: its position and the game time it arrived at, which the
  double-click rule in `specs/controls.md` is measured against.

## The faculty gates

- Whether the automatic turning of an exposed card runs, whether win detection
  runs, whether the cascade's launching runs, and whether the trail's painting
  runs. All four are on when a game begins, and the debugging surface poses them.

## The victory cascade

- The seconds accumulated toward the next launch.
- The number of cards the cascade has launched, which is counted up as cards
  launch rather than derived from the foundations.
- Every card in flight, each with a distinct id, its suit, its rank, its top-left
  position, and its velocity.
- Whether the cascade has finished.
- The number of stamps the painted layer has taken since it was last cleared.

## The rest

- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its delta to whatever
  the screen.
- Whether sound is muted, refreshed from the runtime's mute bit in every update.
- The state of the seeded random generator, which the deal's shuffle and the
  cascade's launch velocities are drawn from.
- Whatever the game needs to hand a fresh id to the next card or flyer it
  creates, so no two entities live at one moment share an id.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so every
field and every array is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the next state from
the current one rather than writing into it, and every reader is handed the state
as `DeepReadonly<CascadeState>` in any case.

```ts
export type Screen = "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "won";

export type Suit = "spades" | "hearts" | "diamonds" | "clubs";

export type PileKind = "stock" | "waste" | "foundation" | "tableau";

export interface CardState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly suit: Suit;
  readonly rank: number;
  readonly faceUp: boolean;
}

export interface DragState {
  readonly cards: readonly CardState[];
  readonly fromPile: "waste" | "foundation" | "tableau";
  readonly fromIndex: number;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
}

export interface DropTargetState {
  readonly pile: "foundation" | "tableau";
  readonly index: number;
}

export interface PointerState {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly down: boolean;
}

export interface PressState {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly at: number;
}

export interface FlyerState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly suit: Suit;
  readonly rank: number;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly vx: number;
  readonly vy: number;
}

export interface CascadeState {
  readonly screen: Screen;
  readonly menuIndex: number;
  readonly titleIndex: number;

  readonly stock: readonly CardState[];
  readonly waste: readonly CardState[];
  readonly wasteSets: readonly number[];
  readonly foundations: readonly (readonly CardState[])[];
  readonly tableau: readonly (readonly CardState[])[];

  readonly drag: DragState | null;
  readonly dropTarget: DropTargetState | null;
  readonly pointer: PointerState;
  readonly lastPress: PressState | null;

  readonly autoFlip: boolean;
  readonly winDetect: boolean;
  readonly launching: boolean;
  readonly trailPainting: boolean;

  readonly launchClock: number;
  readonly launched: number;
  readonly flyers: readonly FlyerState[];
  readonly cascadeDone: boolean;
  readonly trailStamps: number;

  readonly nextId: number;
  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly muted: boolean;
  readonly rngState: number;
}
```
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is the world's game state: a class extending the engine's
`GameState`, named by the game mode as its `gameStateClass`, so the engine builds
one instance of it when the world opens and `engine.world.state` is that
instance. Wherever this specification writes `state.foo`, it means a field of
that instance.

The game runs in one world for the whole session. The game definition registers a
single level, the game never opens another, and every screen is a value of
`screen`, so the state below is built once and lives until the page closes. The
framework's states are live objects: a tick writes the fields it advances in
place, and each field initializer below is that field's title-screen value, the
same value the debug surface's `reset` restores. `GameState` comes from the
engine's package, and `DEFAULT_SEED` from `src/constants.ts`.

`src/constants.ts` fixes the vocabulary the world is built with. The one level is
registered under its name in `LEVELS`, which is the level `startLevel` names, and
every actor drawing the table, the cascade, or the HUD carries its tag from
`TAGS`, so those actors are found with `world.byTag` under the names this
specification uses.

```ts
export type Screen = "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "won";

export type Suit = "spades" | "hearts" | "diamonds" | "clubs";

export type PileKind = "stock" | "waste" | "foundation" | "tableau";

export interface CardState {
  id: number;
  suit: Suit;
  rank: number;
  faceUp: boolean;
}

export interface DragState {
  cards: CardState[];
  fromPile: "waste" | "foundation" | "tableau";
  fromIndex: number;
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

export interface DropTargetState {
  pile: "foundation" | "tableau";
  index: number;
}

export interface PointerState {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  down: boolean;
}

export interface PressState {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  at: number;
}

export interface FlyerState {
  id: number;
  suit: Suit;
  rank: number;
  x: number;
  y: number;
  vx: number;
  vy: number;
}

export class CascadeState extends GameState {
  screen: Screen = "title";
  menuIndex = 0;
  titleIndex = 0;

  stock: CardState[] = [];
  waste: CardState[] = [];
  wasteSets: number[] = [];
  foundations: CardState[][] = [[], [], [], []];
  tableau: CardState[][] = [[], [], [], [], [], [], []];

  drag: DragState | null = null;
  dropTarget: DropTargetState | null = null;
  pointer: PointerState = { x: 0, y: 0, down: false };
  lastPress: PressState | null = null;

  autoFlip = true;
  winDetect = true;
  launching = true;
  trailPainting = true;

  launchClock = 0;
  launched = 0;
  flyers: FlyerState[] = [];
